 PATIENTS AND METHODS
 A retrospective review of 1310 free flaps used for
 head and neck reconstruction in 1266 patients
 between July 1995 and June 2006 at The Univer
sity of Texas M. D. Anderson Cancer Center was
 performed. Patients with intraoperative thrombo
sis that were revised without further consequen
ces were not included. Patients who were taken
 back to surgery because of the loss of a Doppler
 signal without true vascular obstruction werealso
 excluded. The timing of flap compromise, the type
 of vascular occlusion (ie, arterial or venous), the
 causes of vascular occlusion, and the salvage rate
 were recorded and analyzed. This study was
 approvedbytheinstitutionalreview board.
 Typically, patients undergoing major head and
 neck surgery and free flap reconstruction at M. D.
 Anderson Cancer Center during the study period
 were kept in a surgical intensive care unit over
night, sedated, and mechanically ventilated. They
 were awakenedthenext morningandtransferred
 to a head and neck surgery ‘‘flap floor’’ when
 deemed stable. Hourly flap monitoring was per
formed by nurses for the first 3 days. The fre
quency of flap monitoring was reduced to every 2
 hours thereafter for 2 days, then every 4 hours
 until patient discharge. When there was a change
 in skin color or Doppler signal, the resident or
 microsurgery fellow on call was called to assess
 the flap. If the change was indeed suggestive of
 vascular compromise, the patient was taken back
 to the operating room, usually within 1 to 2 hours,
 for exploration.
 In cases with an external skin paddle, flap
 monitoringwasprimarilybasedonclinicalassess
ment. In many cases, however, a hand-held Dopp
ler device such as the Huntleigh Mini Dopplex
 D-900 unit with an 8-MHz probe (Huntleigh
 Diagnostics Ltd., Cardiff, United Kingdom) was
 also used to check the perforator signal on the
 skin surface, which is primarily an arterial signal.
 Some surgeons also used a 20-mHz implantable
 Doppler device (Cook Vascular, Inc., Leechburg,
 PA) to aid in monitoring. In patients with a buried
 f
 lap, the implantable Doppler device was com
monlyusedtomonitortheflap.TheDopplerprobe
 wasplacedontheveininmostcases.
 Statistics. Student’s t test, the chi-square test,
 Fisher’s exact test, and logistic regression analy
ses were used for statistical analysis wherever
 appropriate. A p value of less than .05 was consid
eredstatistically significant.
