 DISCUSSION
 The microsurgery program at M. D. Anderson
 Cancer Center was begun in 1988, and a micro
vascular flap nursing unit was established in
 1994, with continuing training of nurses in flap
 monitoring. Previous reports from our depart
ment6,10 showed pedicle thrombosis and flap fail
ure rates of 6.2% and 4.4%, respectively, in 594
 free flaps for head and neck reconstructions per
formed between 1988 and 1994. The current large
 series beginninginJuly1995showsaflapcompro
mise rate of 3.7% and a flap failure rate of 2%,
 representing a 50% decrease from previous
 reports. This decrease reflects the importance of a
 well-established microsurgery program withmod
ern standards in training, microsurgical skills,
 postoperative flap monitoring, and management
 of microvascular crises. Within the current study
 period of over 11 years, the rates of flap compro
miseandfailurechangedverylittle,evenwithsig
nificant personnel and procedural changes, sug
gesting that such fluctuations are buffered by a
 well-established program. Overall, surgeon expe
rience did not correlate with the rates of flap com
promise and failure, even after excluding the sur
geons who started practicing before 1995 and the
 consultant surgeons whose primary practices
 were outside our institution. However, when the
 surgeons were divided into 2 groups of below or
 above 70 free flaps, the rates of flap compromise
 and failure were twice as high in the ‘‘inexper
ienced’’ surgeons. On the other hand, among the
 ‘‘experienced’’ surgeons, flap compromise and fail
ures occurred randomly and did not show a
 ‘‘learning curve.’’ The surgeons in our department
 typically finished a plastic surgery residency with
 fairly good exposure to microsurgery including
 free flaps and finger replants, followed by a 1-year
 microsurgery fellowship training with an experi
ence of 50 to 80 free flaps. The above findings sug
gest that (1) many surgeons had nearly reached
 the end of their learning curve by the time they
 f
 inished training; (2) skills differ among surgeons;
 and (3) experience counts. As Khouri pointed out,
 1 of the major determinants of the success or fail
ure offree flapprocedures is theoperator and, ina
 broader sense, technical issues.1 Most flap com
promise seen in this study had a clear etiology,
 mainly technical errors, and might be prevent
able. However, we could not identify any particu
lar pattern of technical errors associated with less
 experienced surgeons.
 One unexpected finding from this study was
 that sex wasasignificant predictor of flapcompro
mise and failure, with rates in women being twice
 as high as those in men. The reasons are unclear,
 and this finding should be interpreted with cau
tion. It is possible that because the overall failure
 rate was very low, the study’s statistical power
 maybeinadequate, even with such a large series.
 A literature search did not find similar reports.
 Pregnant women and women taking contracep
tives are known to be associated with higher
 thrombogenic status in general, but these factors
 obviously did not contribute to our series. Previ
ous reports did not show any differences in rates
 of thrombosis and flap failure between male and
 female patients.7,9,11
 The current study also shows that arterial
 thromboses behave differently from venous
 thromboses. Arterial thrombosis tends to occur
 early and is less likely to result in flap salvage. In
 addition, postoperative arterial thrombosis is of
ten associated with intraoperative arterial throm
bosis due to technical difficulties such as artery
 size mismatch, calcified vessels, and technical
 mistakes. Multiple attempts at redoing the anas
tomosis will eventually lead toprolongedischemia
