 We have noticed that the vascular pedicle can be
 quite fixed to the surrounding tissue with the
 patient’s own exudates when left undisturbed for
 an hour or so while insetting the flap. We have
 also found during early postoperative explora
tions, for various reasons, that the vascular pedi
cle is even more firmly fixed and unlikely to be
 twisted or kinked by normal neck movements.
 Theneckisusuallyhyperextendedandsometimes
 rotated to1sideduringreconstruction.Thevascu
lar pedicle mayappearnicelyplacedwiththeneck
 in suchaposition, but once theheadisreturnedto
 a normal position and the superior neck skin flap
 is pulled down for final closure, the vascular pedi
cle is instantly kinked or twisted. This condition is
 often missed because the flap is already covered
 with the neck skin. Therefore, it is extremely im
portant to return the head and neck to a normal
 position first, sometimes with slight flexion, and
 inspect the placement of the vascular pedicle
 again beforeclosure.
 Postoperative thrombosis of the internal jugu
lar vein can obviously cause venous congestion of
 the flapifthevenousoutflowistotheinternaljug
ular vein. The incidence of internal jugular vein
 thrombosis after neck dissection varies from 13%
 to 30%, and it can occur as early as in the first
 postoperative day to several days later.15–21 It is
 rather surprising to see that flap congestion due
 to primary internal jugular vein thrombosis does
 not occur more frequently. The causes for internal
 jugular vein thrombosis are thought to be endo
thelial trauma during surgery, reduced venous
 f
 low (stasis), and altered coagulability profile of
 some cancer patients. Secondary causes may
 include major wound infection, and fistula forma
tion. PulseVac irrigation during incision and
 drainage of neck infection could potentially
 increase the risk of internal jugular vein thrombo
sis as it happened in 1patient, possibly by deliver
ing mechanical force causing diffuse intimal dam
age in the already compromised vessels in the
 zoneofinfection.
 The most common cause of late failures was
 unrecognized vascular crisis. The inaccuracy of
 Doppler examination is well known,22–25 particu
larly in the head and neck region. The abundance
 of blood vessels in the neck and the mobility of the
 neck are possible reasons for the unreliability of
 Doppler examination. The implantable Doppler is
 known to have a sensitivity of 100% but a high
 false-positive rate, ie, loss of Doppler signal in a
 perfectly perfused flap, leading to unnecessary
 surgical explorations.22–24 Two flap failures with
 an audible implantable Doppler signal (false-neg
ative) occurred in this series after the previous
 report from our department.22 The reasons for the
 false-negative readings were unclear. Possible
 explanations include the presence of other blood
 vessels in the close vicinity, improper distance set
tings in the monitoring device, and misinterpreta
tion of the signal. Proper placement of the
 implantable Doppler requires experience. When
ever possible, we prefer externalizing a segment
 of flapformonitoringpurpose.
 In summary, the present study suggests that
 arterial failures are usually fetal and commonly
 caused by technical difficulties at the anastomo
sis, whereas venous failures are most commonly
 the result of mechanical obstruction. The main
 cause of late failures is unrecognized vascular
 events due to the lack of reliable monitoring.
 Therefore, careful planning, flawless execution,
 precise surgical techniques, avoidance of mechan
ical obstruction, and expert monitoring of buried
 f
 laps may further improve the success rate of free
 tissue transfer in complex head and neck onco
logic reconstruction
